414e-06,-4.172316494077677e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,46254,198,59,26056,1242,15159,16,1124,273,502,366,600,92,1124,37018,90,16,15170,17,47822,72,488,502,38154,59,60,2870,279,2629,374,4429,916,678,6785,25780,400,72,3,323,400,73,3,1741,429,400,16,1124,273,502,366,600,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nCompute\n\\[\\sum_{1 \\le j < i} \\frac{1}{2^{i + j}},\\]where the sum is taken over all positive integers $i$ and $j$ such that $1 \\le j < i.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"a1e480bf649c245cf1ae35df0f7604cca1f570f5a9bfd8d8f151b6c15b122cfe"},"seconds":79.47421979904175,"seed":901058994293831421,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the given sum\n\\[\n\\sum_{1 \\le j < i} \\frac{1}{2^{i + j}},\n\\]\nwe first break down the range of summation and the expression inside the summation.\n\nThe sum is over all pairs \\((i, j)\\) where \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) are positive integers and \\(j < i\\). We can rewrite the sum by iterating over \\(i\\) first and then summing over \\(j\\) for each \\(i\\).\n\nFor a fixed \\(i\\), \\(j\\) ranges from 1 to \\(i-1\\). Therefore, we can write the sum as\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^{i+j}}.\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify the inner sum:\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^{i+j}} = \\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^i \\cdot 2^j} = \\frac{1}{2^i} \\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^j}.\n\\]\n\nThe sum \\(\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^j}\\) is a geometric series with the first term \\(a = \\frac{1}{2}\\) and common ratio \\(r = \\frac{1}{2}\\). The sum of the first \\(i-1\\) terms of this geometric series is:\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^j = \\frac{\\frac{1}{2} \\left(1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{i-1}\\right)}{1 - \\frac{1}{2}} = 1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{i-1}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this back into our expression, we get:\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^{i+j}} = \\frac{1}{2^i} \\left(1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{i-1}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2^i} - \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute this result back into the outer sum:\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\left( \\frac{1}{2^i} - \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}} \\right).\n\\]\n\nWe can split this into two separate sums:\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^i} - \\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}}.\n\\]\n\nFirst, consider the sum \\(\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^i}\\):\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^i} = \\frac{1}{2^2} + \\frac{1}{2^3} + \\frac{1}{2^4} + \\cdots = \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{8} + \\frac{1}{16} + \\cdots.\n\\]\n\nThis is a geometric series with the first term \\(a = \\frac{1}{4}\\) and common ratio \\(r = \\frac{1}{2}\\). The sum of this series is:\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^i} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{4}}{1 - \\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{4}}{\\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nNext, consider the sum \\(\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}}\\):\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}} = \\frac{1}{2^3} + \\frac{1}{2^5} + \\frac{1}{2^7} + \\cdots = \\frac{1}{8} + \\frac{1}{32} + \\frac{1}{128} + \\cdots.\n\\]\n\nThis is a geometric series with the first term \\(a = \\frac{1}{8}\\) and common ratio \\(r = \\frac{1}{4}\\). The sum of this series is:\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{8}}{1 - \\frac{1}{4}} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{8}}{\\frac{3}{4}} = \\frac{1}{8} \\times \\frac{4}{3} = \\frac{1}{6}.\n\\]\n\nFinally, we combine the two sums:\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\left( \\frac{1}{2^i} - \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{3}{6} - \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
